      <header class="cod-title-block">
        <p class="cod-chapter-label">Chapter Four · Spirit of Truth or Seducing Spirits?</p>
        <h1 class="cod-title">Translations</h1>
        <p class="cod-subtitle">You Haven't Been Reading the Word</p>
        <p class="cod-tagline">Every English Bible you have ever held comes with fingerprints. You have not been reading Scripture. You have been reading an edition. The Name was erased nearly seven thousand times, and replaced with the title of another god.</p>
      </header>

      <p class="cod-pullquote">Spirit of Truth or Seducing Spirits? Let Scripture and history decide.</p>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">INTRODUCTION</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">My Bible Says…</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">There is a sentence every believer has said and every believer has heard. "My Bible says..." It sounds innocent. It sounds devout. But buried inside that short phrase is an assumption so large that almost no one stops to question it. Which Bible? Translated by whom? From which manuscripts? Under whose theological pressure? Passing through how many centuries of editing, vowel-adding, comma-moving, and name-erasing before landing on the tissue-thin page in your lap?</p>

      <p class="cod-body">The believer assumes his Bible hands him the Word the way the Word was originally given — whole, clean, unchanged. That assumption is almost entirely false. Every translation is a decision. Every decision has a maker. Every maker had a theology. And the English Bible most believers are reading was shaped, in some of its most critical verses, by men who had already absorbed the very doctrines Scripture was supposed to correct — men who were not neutral transmitters but active participants in the religion Scripture has already exposed in the previous three chapters of this series.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Scripture names the power behind what turns believers from the truth: "some shall depart from the faith, giving heed to seducing spirits" (1 Timothy 4:1). Against it stands the Spirit of truth, who "will guide you into all truth" (John 16:13). In translation, the enemy did not need to change a doctrine outright. He only needed to decide, a thousand times over, which word, which vowel, which comma reached the reader's eyes. <strong>In translation, the enemy erased the Name.</strong></p>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">I</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Name Was Given, Not Invented</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">Before there was a translation to bend, there was a Name Yahuah gave on His own authority. At the burning bush He told Mosheh, "This is my name for ever, and this is my memorial unto all generations" (Exodus 3:15). He did not give a title. He did not give a description. He gave a Name, and He attached a promise to it: it would be remembered, spoken, called upon, forever.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">The Name was written, not merely spoken. Mosheh wrote the Torah by Yahuah's own command and placed it beside the Ark (Deuteronomy 31:9, 26). Every letter carried weight; Yahushua Himself said "one jot or one tittle shall in no wise pass from the law, till all be fulfilled" (Matthew 5:18) — a statement about the Hebrew text as it stood in His own day, consonant and vowel together, not about a translation yet to be made. The Son's own Name carries the Father's: Yahushua, given by the messenger to Miryam, means "Yah saves" (Matthew 1:21). The Name was never ornamental. It was the point.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">This is the ground the rest of the chapter stands on. Whatever happened later — vowel points added by men who rejected the Messiah, a sentence inserted into a Latin manuscript, a comma placed by a seventeenth-century committee, a Name replaced nearly seven thousand times with the title of a Canaanite god — all of it happened to a text that began as something Yahuah Himself sealed with His own Name and His own promise that it would be remembered.</p>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">II</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Myth of Direct Transmission</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">Most believers picture the Bible arriving in their hands through something like a straight line. Yahuah inspired the authors. The authors wrote. The text was preserved. The text was translated. The believer reads the result. Simple, linear, faithful.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Reality is nothing like that. The path from the original Hebrew and Greek manuscripts to your English Bible passes through Aramaic Targums, the Septuagint, the Masoretic editors of Tiberias, the Latin Vulgate of Jerome, a thousand years of Catholic monastic copying, the rushed printing of Erasmus, the political theater of the Reformation, and the committees of King James. Each of those steps left fingerprints.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">The Hebrew Old Testament was originally written without vowels. That is not a defect — Hebrew is designed that way. The reader supplies the vowels from knowledge of the spoken language. But it means that when a Hebrew manuscript sat silently in a scroll for a thousand years, the men who later decided which vowels belonged on each word held extraordinary power. They could bend the meaning of an entire passage by the placement of a single dot.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">The Greek New Testament was written in scriptio continua — all capitals, no spaces between words, no punctuation, no paragraph breaks. The men who later decided where one word ended and the next began, where a sentence broke, where a comma fell — those men were not neutral either. Their doctrinal preferences ran downhill into your translation. And a single comma can change a whole doctrine, as will be seen in a moment.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">This is the pattern a seducing spirit always uses in this chapter: it does not need to rewrite a page. It only needs to stand quietly at every joint where a decision had to be made — a vowel, a word break, a comma — so the reader never thinks to ask who held the pen.</p>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">III</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Masoretic Bent (Sixth to Tenth Century A.D.)</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">The Old Testament your pastor preaches from is almost certainly translated from a source called the Masoretic Text. The name sounds ancient and authoritative. Most believers assume it is the Hebrew Bible that has existed unchanged since the days of the prophets. It is not.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">The Masoretic Text was produced between the sixth and tenth centuries A.D. — between five hundred and nine hundred years after the resurrection — by a school of Hebrew scribes known as the Masoretes. These men were not descendants of the Temple priesthood. They were not the heirs of the apostles. They were Jewish rabbis who had firmly rejected Yahushua as Messiah and were preserving the Hebrew Scriptures within a religion defined, in part, against Him.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Their most consequential decision was the introduction of vowel points — small dots and dashes added above and below the Hebrew consonants to fix the pronunciation of every word forever. Before their work, Hebrew was a sea of consonants that could flex. After their work, every word had a locked-in vowel pattern. A system invented a thousand years after the last prophet spoke now decided how every ambiguous word would be read forever.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Most of the time this was harmless. Sometimes it was devastating. When a word could legitimately be read two different ways depending on the vowels or a single letter — when one reading pointed toward the Messiah and the other reading away from Him — the Masoretes made a choice. They were not neutral. They were theologically committed opponents of the One that text was written to describe. And the pattern is not one verse. It is everywhere. Here is a short tour.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Psalm 22:16 — "they pierced" becomes "like a lion." The most famous example. The Masoretic reads ka'ari — "like a lion my hands and feet" (which makes no grammatical sense as a sentence). The older Dead Sea Scrolls, the Septuagint, and the Peshitta all read ka'aru — "they pierced my hands and feet." The difference is a single letter. A Dead Sea Scroll fragment from Nachal Hever, dated A.D. 50-68, nearly nine hundred years older than the Masoretic Aleppo Codex, clearly shows the "pierced" reading. The Masoretic reading buries a direct crucifixion prophecy.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Genesis 47:31 — "bed" becomes "staff." Same three consonants: mth. Vowel-pointed mittah means "bed." Vowel-pointed matteh means "staff" or "rod." The Masoretic says Jacob "bowed himself upon the bed's head." But Hebrews 11:21 quotes the verse as Jacob worshipping "leaning on the top of his staff" (following the Septuagint). The New Testament writer used the Septuagint reading, not the Masoretic. One is a sick old man propped up in bed; the other is a patriarch standing in worship on his rod of authority.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Job 13:15 — "I will trust" becomes "I will NOT trust." The famous verse: "Though He slay me, yet will I trust Him." The Hebrew has identical pronunciation for two opposite words: lo (not) and lo (in Him). Same sound, opposite meaning. The written form is "not." The read-aloud form is "in Him." This same swap happens in at least fifteen other places across the Tanakh. The Masoretes had to make a decision every time. There is no guarantee they always made the right one.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Genesis 49:10 — "Shiloh" becomes "that which is his." "The scepter shall not depart from Judah... until Shiloh come." But the consonants can also be read shello — "that which is his" or "to whom it belongs." Ezekiel 21:27 echoes this with "until he come whose right it is." Either reading is Messianic, but they paint different pictures — one is a proper name, the other is a description of the rightful owner of the throne. The Masoretic picks the obscure name and hides the ownership claim.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Hosea 14:2 — "bulls" becomes "fruit of our lips." The Masoretic reads parim — "so will we render the calves of our lips." The Septuagint reads peri — "fruit of our lips." Hebrews 13:15 quotes this verse as "the sacrifice of praise... that is, the fruit of our lips." The New Testament writer used the Septuagint reading, not the Masoretic. The Masoretic is incoherent ("calves of our lips"?). The Septuagint is what the apostles actually read.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Deuteronomy 32:8 — "sons of Israel" becomes "sons of God." The Masoretic: "He set the bounds of the people according to the number of the children of Israel." The Dead Sea Scrolls and the Septuagint: "according to the number of the sons of God." This is enormous. It changes the whole framework of how the nations were divided, and connects directly to Psalm 82 and the divine council passages modern Christianity tends to spiritualize away. The Masoretic edit removes the heavenly beings from the text.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Six examples. There are more than a thousand. The pattern repeats. Most often, when the New Testament quotes the Old Testament, it quotes the Septuagint — and when the Septuagint disagrees with the Masoretic, the New Testament usually matches the Septuagint. Hebrews quotes the Septuagint. Matthew quotes the Septuagint. Paul quotes the Septuagint. Not every time — Matthew 2:15 and John 19:37 follow the Hebrew — but the pattern is plain. The apostles' Bible was not the Masoretic. They were reading an older, less-edited version.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">This is not a coincidence and it is not a technicality. It is a pattern. Tenth-century men who rejected the Messiah had the power to bend how every English speaker would read the Old Testament a thousand years later. The consonantal text is generally trustworthy. The vowel points are man's tradition. And in some places that tradition carried an agenda. This is the work of a seducing spirit: letting men who had rejected the Messiah decide, a thousand years later, which reading of a messianic prophecy the world would inherit.</p>

      <details class="cod-deepdive">
        <summary>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-label">
            <span class="cod-deepdive-icon">✦</span>
            <span class="cod-deepdive-title">Go Deeper — The Dead Sea Scrolls and the Older Reading</span>
          </span>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-chevron">▾</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="cod-deepdive-body">
          <p class="cod-body">In 1947 a Bedouin shepherd named Muhammed edh-Dhib, searching for a lost goat near the ruins of Qumran on the western shore of the Dead Sea, threw a stone into a cave opening and heard pottery break. Inside were clay jars holding leather and papyrus scrolls that had sat undisturbed since before the destruction of the Second Temple. Over the following decade, eleven caves yielded roughly 900 manuscripts, among them fragments of every Old Testament book except Esther.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">The scrolls were copied between roughly 250 B.C. and A.D. 68, which places them anywhere from five hundred to twelve hundred years earlier than the Masoretic Text. For the first time, scholars could compare the Hebrew Bible the Masoretes produced against Hebrew manuscripts that predated the Masoretes by a millennium. The comparison mattered because it was no longer a question of the Septuagint, a Greek translation, against the Masoretic, a Hebrew text — it was Hebrew against Hebrew, old against new, with no language barrier to argue away.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">The find confirmed that the Masoretic Text is, on the whole, a careful and faithful copy of a very old tradition — the overall agreement between the Qumran scrolls and the Masoretic Text is high, and that fact deserves to be said plainly. But in the specific places where the Masoretic reading differs from the Septuagint, the Dead Sea Scrolls repeatedly side with the Septuagint and against the Masoretic. Psalm 22:16 is the clearest single case: a fragment catalogued from the Nachal Hever caves, south of Qumran and dated to the first century, preserves the letter that the Masoretic Aleppo Codex, copied roughly nine hundred years later, does not.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">Deuteronomy 32:8 is the other landmark case. A Qumran fragment of Deuteronomy, identified as 4QDeutq, reads "sons of God" (bene elohim) exactly where the Masoretic reads "sons of Israel" (bene yisrael) — matching the Septuagint against the later Hebrew tradition. The scrolls did not invent a new reading. They preserved an older one that the Masoretes, centuries later and for reasons of their own, moved away from.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">The discovery did not hand believers a brand-new Bible. It handed them a window into the centuries before the Masoretes, and in that window, a pattern already visible from the Septuagint stood confirmed in Hebrew: on the handful of verses that carry the most theological weight, the oldest surviving witness and the Greek translation agree with each other and disagree with the text most English Bibles are built on.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <p class="cod-body">This is a short sample of a very large pattern. Every comma in your English New Testament was placed by a man who had a theology. When you see a verse that seems to neatly prove a doctrine modern Christianity holds dear, the first question to ask is whether the Greek actually required that reading — or whether a translator's pen decided for you.</p>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">IV</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Vulgate's Inserted Trinity (A.D. 382-1522)</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">If the Masoretic Text is the shadow over the Old Testament, the Latin Vulgate is the shadow over the New. Between A.D. 382 and 405, a man named Jerome produced a Latin translation of the Bible under a commission from Pope Damasus I — the same imperial church system that produced the canon lists of Hippo and Carthage. For the next thousand years, the Vulgate was the Bible in the West.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Most of it is a reasonable translation. But a small number of its verses were either flatly invented or heavily bent to support doctrines the original manuscripts do not support. The most notorious example is 1 John 5:7 — the single most explicit "Trinity verse" in your Bible:</p>

      <p class="cod-body">1 John 5:7, as printed in the King James: "For there are three that bear record in heaven, the Father, the Word, and the Holy Ghost: and these three are one."</p>

      <p class="cod-body">What the earliest Greek manuscripts actually say: the entire phrase is absent. Verse 7 reads simply, "For there are three that bear witness — the Spirit, and the water, and the blood; and these three agree as one."</p>

      <p class="cod-body">The italicized phrase — known as the Johannine Comma — does not appear in any Greek manuscript of 1 John before the fourteenth century. Not one. It appears first in the Latin writings of Priscillian around 380, then in Vulgate manuscripts from about the seventh century — it is missing from the oldest complete Vulgate New Testament, Codex Fuldensis (546) — and carried forward by Rome for a thousand years. Erasmus, compiling the first printed Greek New Testament in the sixteenth century, originally left it out — because it was not in any Greek manuscript he had. Then a Greek manuscript containing the verse — Codex Montfortianus, copied around 1520 — was put in front of him, and he included it in his third edition of 1522. The Greek witness was made in his own lifetime to supply the verse. That third edition is the source text behind the Textus Receptus, which is the source text behind the King James, which is the source text behind the doctrine that sits in most evangelical pews today.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">The clearest proof-text for the Trinity in the English Bible is an insertion. Not a translation. Not a rendering. An insertion — a phrase added centuries after the apostle died, by scribes serving a theological system the apostle never taught. The question is unavoidable: if the Father required a sentence to be added to His Word in order for believers to find the Trinity in it, which spirit did that addition serve? A seducing spirit does not need to rewrite a whole Bible; one inserted sentence, defended for a thousand years, is enough.</p>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">V</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">When a Comma Becomes a Doctrine</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">The Johannine Comma is not the only place a translator's punctuation decision carries a doctrine. Because the original Greek had no punctuation whatsoever, every comma, every period, every question mark in your New Testament was added by a man. And in at least two places those decisions do not just affect grammar — they invent doctrines.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Luke 23:43, in the original Greek with no punctuation: "Truly I say to you today you will be with Me in Paradise." The printed reading, which supports the "immediate conscious afterlife" doctrine: "Truly I say to you, today you will be with Me in Paradise." The alternative reading, a simple emphatic promise: "Truly I say to you today, you will be with Me in Paradise."</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Same words. Same Greek. One comma, different position. The first reading tells the thief on the stake he will be conscious in Paradise that very day — and has been used for centuries to prop up the Greek-philosophy doctrine of the immortal soul going to heaven at the moment of death. The second reading is an emphatic promise: I am telling you, right now, that you will be with Me — without specifying when Paradise arrives. Scripture teaches the dead sleep until the resurrection (Daniel 12:2; John 11:11-14; 1 Thessalonians 4:16). If the second reading is correct, the most famous afterlife proof-text in the Bible is not about an afterlife at all. One translator's comma decides which doctrine the reader walks away with.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Romans 9:5, in the original Greek with no punctuation: "...of whom is the Messiah according to the flesh who is over all God blessed forever amen." The printed, Trinitarian reading: "...of whom is the Messiah according to the flesh, who is over all, God blessed forever, amen." A second reading, a doxology to the Father: "...of whom is the Messiah according to the flesh, who is over all. God be blessed forever, amen." A third reading, separating Messiah from God: "...of whom is the Messiah according to the flesh. God, who is over all, be blessed forever, amen."</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Same Greek. Three completely different theologies. The printed punctuation happens to equate the Messiah with God — conveniently supporting the Trinity doctrine the translators were already committed to. The other two readings treat the final clause as a praise of the Father, distinct from the Son just mentioned — which is how Paul writes consistently everywhere else, and which lines up with the Hebrew Scriptures where Yahuah is one and the Messiah is His Anointed. The translator had to choose. The choice happened to favor the doctrine already in his hand. The Spirit of truth needs no comma to make its point; a seducing spirit depends on exactly one.</p>

      <details class="cod-deepdive">
        <summary>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-label">
            <span class="cod-deepdive-icon">✦</span>
            <span class="cod-deepdive-title">Go Deeper — Erasmus, the Textus Receptus, and the Rushed King James</span>
          </span>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-chevron">▾</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="cod-deepdive-body">
          <p class="cod-body">In 1516, a Dutch scholar named Desiderius Erasmus produced the first printed Greek New Testament. He was in a race with a Spanish project that had beaten him to manuscript-collecting but not to the press. Erasmus worked so quickly that his first edition is widely considered one of the most poorly edited books of its era. He had access to fewer than a dozen late Greek manuscripts, most from the twelfth century or later — not originals, not early copies, but medieval handwritten volumes.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">For the last six verses of Revelation (22:16-21), Erasmus did not have any Greek manuscript. He filled the gaps by back-translating from the Latin Vulgate into his own Greek. That back-translated Greek — Erasmus's best guess at what the Greek might have said — ended up in his printed edition. From there, it passed into every subsequent revision of what became the Textus Receptus — the "Received Text" — which became the standard Greek New Testament of Protestantism for the next three centuries.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">The Textus Receptus is the text underneath every major English Bible from Tyndale through the Geneva Bible and into the 1611 King James. When the King James translators sat down to work, they were not translating from pristine apostolic Greek. They were translating from Erasmus's rushed edition, heavily stitched together, and in several passages literally retro-translated from Latin. Every "King James only" argument collapses under the simple question: which edition?</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">VI</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Missing Name, and Its Replacement</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">If there is one fingerprint that exposes everything else, it is this one. Open almost any English Bible, turn to the Old Testament, and scan for the word LORD — printed in small capital letters. You will see it everywhere. The sacred Name stands about 6,828 times in the Hebrew, and the King James replaces it with LORD or GOD in all but a handful. And in almost every one of those occurrences, the underlying Hebrew is not the word for "lord" at all. It is the four letters of the sacred name: YHWH — the covenant name of the Creator.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">The translators did not render the name. They erased it. Nearly seven thousand times, the personal covenant name of the Creator was removed from the text of the Old Testament and replaced with a title. The name Yahuah gave Mosheh at the burning bush — the name He said would be His memorial unto all generations (Exodus 3:15) — was crossed out seven thousand times.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">The decision traces back to a superstition that entered Judaism in the centuries before the Messiah, in which the divine name was considered too holy to pronounce. The Masoretes, when they added their vowel points, deliberately inserted the vowels of the Hebrew word Adonai ("lord") underneath the consonants YHWH — a signal to the reader not to read the name but to substitute the title. The Septuagint translators continued the substitution with Kyrios. Jerome used Dominus. By the time the text reached English, the name had been buried under three layers of replacement titles.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Most believers go their entire lives without pronouncing the name of the One they claim to worship. They pray to the Lord. They sing about the Lord. They read about the Lord. But Yahuah said it is by His name He is to be known. Not by a title. Not by a euphemism. By the name.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">The same pattern shows up in miniature with the Messiah. He was born into a Hebrew-speaking family. His name, given by the messenger to Miryam, was Yahushua — meaning "Yah is salvation." The Greek rendering is Iesous. The Latin is Iesus. Early English manuscripts rendered it Iesus or Iesu. The letter J did not exist as a separate letter in English until the sixteenth century, and the 1611 King James itself printed the name Iesus. The spelling "Jesus" came after it. The J sound is older — Middle English already said Jhesu, borrowed from French — but either way the sound moved further still from the Hebrew. The spelling "Jesus" is younger than the King James Bible. The One whose Hebrew name means Yahuah saves had that linguistic testimony erased from His own title. Yah-ushua became Jesus.</p>

      <h3 class="cod-subsection">The Replacement Word Is Not Innocent</h3>

      <p class="cod-body">Now here is the part most believers have never been shown, and it is the part that changes the picture from "careless substitution" to something far darker. The word they replaced the Father's name with — Lord — is the functional equivalent, in semantic meaning, of the Hebrew word Baal.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">In Hebrew, Baal literally means "lord, master, owner, husband." That is the exact range covered by the English word Lord and the Greek Kyrios. The Canaanites called their god "the Baal" the same way the English call their god "the Lord." It was their title for their deity. And then the translators of our English Bible reached into every passage where Yahuah's personal name appeared — and replaced it with the English equivalent of the title Canaan was using for a different god entirely.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Do not take this as a guess. Yahuah addressed this very substitution through the prophet Hosea, in one of the sharpest passages in the entire Hebrew Bible: "And it shall be at that day, saith Yahuah, that thou shalt call Me Ishi (my husband); and shalt call Me no more Baali (my lord)" (Hosea 2:16).</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Read that again. Yahuah Himself commanded His people to stop calling Him by a word that doubles as the title for a Canaanite god — because using the same term for both blends them in the worshipper's mind. The prophet is warning against exactly the substitution the translators later performed 6,828 times. The name Yahuah told His people to remember forever — replaced with a word He specifically told them to stop using for Him.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">This was not an accident. A translator sitting at his desk in the seventeenth century, making a choice between rendering the covenant name and substituting a generic title, had many options. He chose the one word Yahuah had explicitly rejected. Whether he knew it or not, his pen executed the merger Hosea was commanded to prevent. Every English Bible that reads "Lord" in place of Yahuah is, in the technical Hebrew sense, reading "Baal." If you cannot find His name, you cannot call on His name. And the translators made sure you could not find it — by substituting the exact word He told Hosea never to use. This is the clearest mark of a seducing spirit in the entire chapter: it did not merely mistranslate the Name, it replaced it with the title of the very god Yahuah told Hosea to stop naming Him by.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">The Father's name erased nearly seven thousand times. The replacement word was the one He already rejected.</p>

      <details class="cod-deepdive">
        <summary>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-label">
            <span class="cod-deepdive-icon">✦</span>
            <span class="cod-deepdive-title">Go Deeper — Tyndale, the Burned Translator</span>
          </span>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-chevron">▾</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="cod-deepdive-body">
          <p class="cod-body">William Tyndale began his English New Testament in the 1520s determined that the plowboy in the English countryside should be able to read Scripture in his own tongue as readily as the priest read it in Latin. The Catholic hierarchy of his day forbade an English Bible outright; Tyndale worked in exile, first in Germany and then in Antwerp, smuggling printed copies back into England inside bales of cloth.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">His word choices were deliberate acts of theology. Where the Latin tradition and the church hierarchy expected priest, Tyndale wrote elder. Where they expected church, with its weight of institution and hierarchy, Tyndale wrote congregation — a plain word for a gathered people, closer to the sense of the Greek ekklesia, a called-out assembly, than the loaded English word that eventually displaced it. Where the tradition expected penance, Tyndale wrote repentance. Each substitution quietly removed a plank of the system that had grown up around the Latin text.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">The church hierarchy did not treat these as neutral translation choices. Tyndale's English New Testament was condemned and publicly burned in London within a year of its printing. Officials under Cardinal Wolsey and then under Thomas More hunted for copies and for the man himself. In 1535 Tyndale was betrayed by a man he trusted, Henry Phillips, arrested in Antwerp, and imprisoned for over a year in Vilvoorde Castle outside Brussels.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">On the sixth of October, 1536, Tyndale was tied to a stake, strangled, and his body burned, on the order of authorities acting under the Holy Roman Empire with the approval of the English crown of the day. He is reported to have cried out at the end, "Lord, open the King of England's eyes." Within three years, an English Bible was authorized for use in every parish in England — built substantially on Tyndale's own translation work, carried forward under a different name.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">King James's translators, working eight decades later, inherited Tyndale's basic text and his translation instincts for roughly eighty to ninety percent of the New Testament's wording — and then overruled him on exactly the words the earlier hierarchy had objected to. Where Tyndale wrote congregation, King James's instructions required church. The man who died so that English readers could hold the Word in their own hands did not get to decide what some of its most load-bearing words would say.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <h3 class="cod-subsection">"Church" for Ekklesia</h3>

      <p class="cod-body">The Greek word in every New Testament reference is ekklesia — a called-out assembly. Tyndale, as the panel above shows, chose congregation, accurately capturing the sense. King James's translators deliberately overruled him. They were instructed to use church. The English word derives from kyriakon — "belonging to the lord" — a word that functioned in the pagan world before it attached itself to Christian use. Every time a believer says "I'm going to church," he is using a word Scripture does not.</p>

      <details class="cod-deepdive">
        <summary>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-label">
            <span class="cod-deepdive-icon">✦</span>
            <span class="cod-deepdive-title">Go Deeper — The Fingerprints: Words That Got Bent</span>
          </span>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-chevron">▾</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="cod-deepdive-body">
          <p class="cod-body">"Easter" in Acts 12:4. The King James contains one of the most blatant pagan insertions in any English translation. Acts 12:4 reads that Herod intended to kill Peter "after Easter." The Greek word in the manuscript is pascha — the Passover. Every other occurrence of pascha in the New Testament is correctly translated Passover. In Acts 12:4 alone, the translators replaced the Hebraic feast with the name of a pagan fertility goddess. Modern translations mostly corrected this — but the King James carried it for four hundred years, and millions of believers still argue Easter has biblical sanction because of one verse that was mistranslated on purpose.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">"Hell" — four words collapsed into one. The English word hell in the King James does the work of four distinct original-language words: Sheol (Hebrew, the grave), Hades (the Greek equivalent), Gehenna (a literal valley outside Jerusalem used as a metaphor for final destruction), and Tartaroo (the holding place for rebellious heavenly beings in 2 Peter 2:4). The translators collapsed all four into a single English word. The result is a "hell" doctrine stitched together from four different source-words — and then read back into the text as if it had always been one concept.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">"Grace" divorced from covenant. The Hebrew chen and Greek charis both carry the sense of unearned favor shown within a covenant relationship. Grace in Scripture is always toward a people defined by the covenant, not a release from the covenant. A believer hearing "we're under grace, not law" from his pulpit is hearing English with four centuries of Reformation baggage pasted onto a word that originally meant something very different.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">VII</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">Reading Past the Filter</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">None of this means the Bible is thrown away. The Word of Yahuah is still in there. The Hebrew behind the Old Testament is still His word. The Greek behind the New Testament is still the witness of the apostles. The translators did not destroy the text — they filtered it. The task is to learn what the filter changed, and to read through it instead of being trapped behind it.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">A handful of practical habits make most of the fingerprints visible. Look up the original words. A Hebrew or Greek interlinear and a basic lexicon are now free and instant online. Any word that carries theological weight in a given Bible deserves to be checked against its Hebrew or Greek root. Compare translations. Not to find the "right" one — no such translation exists — but to see where the translators disagreed with each other, because those are the seams where the decisions were made. Restore the names. Every time the Bible says the LORD, read Yahuah. Every time it says Jesus, know the name He was actually given was Yahushua. Let the Hebrew speak underneath the English.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">And always measure the New against the Old. The Torah, the Prophets, and the Writings were the only Scriptures Yahushua and the apostles ever referred to as Scripture. They are the foundation. The New Testament is the witness of those who walked with the Messiah, but the witness has passed through the editors' hands far more times than the foundation has. The Spirit of truth sends the reader back to the Hebrew and the Name; a seducing spirit is content to leave him trusting the filter.</p>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part cod-part--plain"><h2 class="cod-part-title">What Scripture Shows Instead</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">This is the reading the Spirit of truth teaches.</p>

      <ul class="cod-list">
        <li><strong>The Name stands forever.</strong> "This is my name for ever, and this is my memorial unto all generations" (Exodus 3:15).</li>
        <li><strong>Not one letter is small.</strong> "One jot or one tittle shall in no wise pass from the law, till all be fulfilled" (Matthew 5:18).</li>
        <li><strong>The word is magnified above the Name itself.</strong> "I will worship toward thy holy temple, and praise thy name for thy lovingkindness and for thy truth: for thou hast magnified thy word above all thy name" (Psalm 138:2).</li>
        <li><strong>Nothing is added, nothing is taken away.</strong> "Ye shall not add unto the word which I command you, neither shall ye diminish ought from it" (Deuteronomy 4:2); "If any man shall add unto these things... and if any man shall take away from the words of the book" (Revelation 22:18-19).</li>
        <li><strong>Whoever calls on the Name is saved.</strong> "Whosoever shall call on the name of Yahuah shall be delivered" (Joel 2:32); "whosoever shall call on the name of the Lord shall be saved" (Acts 2:21).</li>
        <li><strong>The foundation does not move.</strong> "To the law and to the testimony: if they speak not according to this word, it is because there is no light in them" (Isaiah 8:20).</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part cod-part--plain"><h2 class="cod-part-title">What Is the Harm?</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">Paul said the fight is "against principalities, against powers" (Ephesians 6:12). A principality does not need to burn every Bible or outlaw every translation. It only needs the believer to stop asking who held the pen. A believer who trusts the committee, the vowel points, and the comma without ever checking them against the Hebrew and the Greek underneath is still reading his Bible faithfully, by his own lights. But he has handed his theology to men he has never met, some of whom had already rejected the very Messiah the text was written to reveal — and the Name he was told to call on has been buried under three languages of substitute titles he was never taught to look past.</p>

      <p class="cod-pullquote"><strong>The enemy does not need to destroy the Word. He only needs the reader to mistake the filter for the text, and the title for the Name.</strong></p>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part cod-part--plain"><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Verdict</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">Put the pieces next to each other. The Masoretic scribes chose readings — a vowel here, a letter there — that bent messianic prophecies away from the Messiah, and the pattern repeats across hundreds of verses, with the New Testament itself quoting the older Septuagint readings rather than theirs, a pattern the Dead Sea Scrolls later confirmed in Hebrew. The Latin Vulgate carried an inserted Trinity verse that no apostle ever wrote, and Erasmus, who dedicated his Greek New Testament to the pope, put it back in once a freshly made Greek copy was handed to him. A single comma in Luke 23 built a doctrine of immediate conscious afterlife the Hebrew Scriptures never taught. A single comma in Romans 9 quietly equates the Son with the Father in a way Paul does not write anywhere else. A translator who gave his life so Englishmen could read the Word in their own tongue was overruled, after his death, on the very words his hierarchy had objected to. And the covenant name of the Creator Himself, erased 6,828 times, was replaced with the English equivalent of the very word Yahuah told Hosea to stop using for Him.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">None of these fingerprints are accidents. Each one points in the same direction — away from the Hebraic foundation, away from the Father's name, away from the Messiah's Hebrew testimony, and toward the doctrines a Gentile Roman religion needed in order to function on its own terms. The record runs one way: from a Name given as a memorial forever, to a vowel point, a comma, and a committee standing between the reader and that Name. That is the direction a seducing spirit moves. In translation, the enemy erased the Name.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Revelation shows the Name is not lost forever. The rider on the white horse "had a name written, that no man knew, but he himself... And he was clothed with a vesture dipped in blood: and his name is called The Word of God" (Revelation 19:12-13). The Word and the Name stand together at the end exactly as they stood together at the beginning, before any vowel point, any comma, or any committee came between them.</p>

      <p class="cod-pullquote"><strong>The Word is still there. Read past the filter, and call on the Name.</strong></p>

      <div class="cod-end"><span class="cod-end-mark">◆</span></div>
    </div>
